## Test Smell Audit — Academic Catalog (testsmells.org)

**Target:** `DataAccess.IntegrationTests/UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`
**Framework:** MSTest 4.1 · .NET 10 · `[TestCategory("Integration")]`

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High | 3 | 3 |
| Medium | 1 | 1 |
| Low | 0 | 0 |
| **Total** | **4** | **4** |

---

### Findings by Severity

---

#### 🔴 HIGH — Sleepy Test (Smell #3)

**Method:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000); // ← blocks 3 seconds, non-deterministic
```

**Why it matters (even in integration tests):** `Thread.Sleep` introduces non-determinism — the 3 s may be too short under CI load (false failure) or too long under normal conditions (slow suite). Integration calibration rules *downgrade* Mystery Guest / Eager Test for integration suites, but Sleepy Test remains high because a poll/retry pattern is always superior.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    // Poll with timeout instead of sleeping
    var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow.AddSeconds(5);
    while (!_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
    {
        Assert.IsTrue(DateTime.UtcNow < deadline, "Notification not sent within timeout.");
        await Task.Delay(100);
    }
    Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky CI failures; artificially slow test suite.

---

#### 🔴 HIGH — Conditional Test Logic (Smell #1)

**Method:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (lines 101–108)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Why it matters:** One branch will *never* execute for the given setup data. The test silently passes without exercising the "PremiumUser" path — or vice-versa — creating a hidden testing gap.

**Fix:** Split into two explicit tests:
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandard_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenPremium_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("prem@example.com", "Prem", 0.15m));
    var loaded = (PremiumUser)_repository.GetByEmail("prem@example.com")!;

    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — one branch never tested; bug could hide for months.

---

#### 🔴 HIGH — Assertion-Free Test / Unknown Test (Smell #4)

**Method:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (lines 113–119)

```csharp
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
    // ← No assertion — passes even if every insert silently fails
}
```

**Why it matters:** MSTest marks this as "passed" as long as no exception is thrown. If `Insert` swallows errors or silently truncates data, you'll never know.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Complete false confidence — 0% defect detection capability.

---

#### 🟡 MEDIUM — Assertion Roulette (Extended Catalog)

**Method:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (lines 35–38)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

4 assertions with **no message parameters**. When one fails in CI, the stack trace shows only a generic "Assert.AreEqual failed. Expected:<Alice>. Actual:<null>" without context.

**Fix:** Add discriminating messages:
```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should survive round-trip");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should survive round-trip");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Id should be assigned by the database");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower triage when tests fail in CI — developer must read source to locate the failing assertion.

---

### Smell-Free Patterns ✅

The following tests are **well-designed** for their integration scope:

| Test | Why it's good |
|------|--------------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Clear Arrange/Act/Assert, focused scope, meaningful assertions |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Single behavior, proper negative assertion (`Assert.IsNull`) |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Count assertion is contextually obvious (3 inserts → 3 count) |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Tests real concurrency concern, clear assertion |

The **fixture design** (`[TestInitialize]` with in-memory SQLite) is also excellent — hermetic, fast, no Mystery Guest, and both fields are used by every test (no General Fixture smell).

---

### Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Effort | Action |
|----------|-------|--------|--------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | 2 min | Add `Assert.AreEqual(100, ...)` to `BulkInsert` |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | 10 min | Split into two focused tests with explicit setup |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | 10 min | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with poll-with-timeout |
| 4 | Assertion Roulette | 5 min | Add message strings to multi-assert methods |

**Bottom line:** 4 out of 8 tests are clean. The 3 high-severity smells are classic, well-known anti-patterns with straightforward fixes. Addressing priorities 1–3 will eliminate all false-confidence and flakiness risks.
